To speed up provisioning without relying on custom cloud snapshots or golden images, you need to eliminate the biggest bottlenecks in a fresh boot: package manager downloads, sequential polling, and redundant OS tasks.
Here are the most effective ways to cut provisioning time down from several minutes to under 60–90 seconds on stock Linux images:

1. Parallelise Node Provisioning & Decouple Dependencies

• Boot all nodes simultaneously: Instead of waiting for the database VPS to finish booting before triggering the API calls to create the autoscaler and webservers, fire all VM creation requests in parallel (& + wait). Cloud providers take 20–40 seconds just to allocate virtual hardware; overlapping those allocations saves nearly a minute immediately.
• Let nodes install OS packages while waiting: Even if a webserver needs database credentials before configuring WordPress/Tomcat, let it run apt-get update && apt-get install concurrently while the database boots, then pause only at the exact step where DB_HOST is required.

2. Strip Down apt / Package Installation Overhead

On Debian/Ubuntu VPS instances, apt-get often accounts for 60% of cloud-init runtime due to man-page generation, recommended packages, and interactive prompts.
• Skip recommended packages and docs: Pass --no-install-recommends to every apt-get install command and set DEBIAN_FRONTEND=noninteractive. This frequently cuts download and extraction sizes by 40–50%.
• Batch all package installs into a single command: Running apt-get install -y pkg1 pkg2 pkg3 once avoids acquiring dpkg locks, updating initramfs, and rebuilding man-db/triggers multiple times across separate scripts.
• Disable man-db and apt translations during bootstrap: Dropping a small config snippet in /etc/dpkg/dpkg.cfg.d/01_nodoc (excluding /usr/share/doc/* and /usr/share/man/*) and /etc/apt/apt.conf.d/99nocache (Acquire::Languages "none";) drastically reduces disk I/O on small VPS disks.

3. Pre-Bundle Compiled Artifacts & Dependencies

Rather than compiling tools or downloading dozens of small files over the internet on every boot:
• Build-machine artifact caching: Have the build machine download or pre-package vendor dependencies (such as Composer vendor/ directories, PHP extensions, or linode-cli wheels) once, and push a single compressed archive (zstd or tar.gz) to new nodes over the fast private VPC network right after their SSH callback fires.
• Shallow Git clones: Ensure every git clone inside cloud-init uses --depth 1 --single-branch so nodes only download the latest commit rather than the full repository history.

4. Replace Sleep-Polling with Event Callbacks & Tighter Loops

• Eliminate fixed sleep delays: Replace arbitrary sleep 30 or sleep 60 pauses in build scripts with tight 1–2 second readiness loops (while [ ! -f ... ]; do sleep 1; done) driven by your new ReceiveCallback.sh SSH callbacks.
• Local readiness checks: Have services poll localhost sockets (mysqladmin ping, curl -sf http://127.0.0.1:8080, or ss -tlpn) every second rather than waiting on external DNS or object-store propagation.

5. Skip Unnecessary Post-Boot Tasks on Ephemeral Nodes

• Defer apt-get upgrade: On short-lived autoscaled webservers, running a full apt-get dist-upgrade on every boot can add 60–120 seconds (especially if it triggers a kernel or grub update). Apply security updates selectively (unattended-upgrades in the background after the webserver is already serving traffic) rather than blocking initial boot.
